        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-86dea678c8044a4f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"Birkin’s “I love you” is primarily a sincere avowal of love and also participates in his acceptance of Ursula as his partner and in their imminent marriage. Its whispering, physical intensity, and trembling establish passionate delivery but do not independently make the words an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The narration says that the conventional formula inadequately expresses Birkin’s experience of transformed, impersonal union. This complicates what his avowal means, but does not establish a distinct core force outside T and P.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly whispers “I love you” to Ursula while kissing her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs after Birkin tells Ursula that she will stay with him and that they are already effectively married. The narration presents his words as truthful and deeply meant, associating them with gratitude, renewed life, and acceptance of Ursula as his mate. Ursula nevertheless wants more explicit proof, while the narrator stresses that “I love you” cannot fully communicate Birkin’s vision of their transcendent oneness. That representational inadequacy concerns the extraordinary state behind the utterance rather than requiring another category of core speech-act force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he whispered as he kissed her, and trembled with pure hope.\",\"supports\":\"The direct wording and narration strongly support a loving-state avowal; whispering and trembling indicate intensity but not independently E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Birkin has just said, “Here, with me” and “We’re married as much today as we shall be tomorrow.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate relational setting gives the avowal substantial undertaking force connected with living together and marriage.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“She could not know how much it meant to him, how much he meant by the few words.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly presents the utterance as deeply intended rather than rote, ironic, or merely instrumental.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration calls his later repetition “whispering with truth” but says that “I love you” was “not the real truth,” because his experience was “something beyond love” and beyond separate “I” and “you.”\",\"supports\":\"This supports sincerity while explaining the formula’s limited ability to convey his larger metaphysical experience; it creates interpretive pressure but not ontology failure at the level of core force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“This marriage with her was his resurrection and his life,” and they are legally married the next day.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal belongs to a concrete mutual-union and marriage context, strengthening P alongside T.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter XXVII of D. H. Lawrence’s Women in Love.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its canonical location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the utterance as both avowal and relationship-affirming undertaking. Lawrence’s account of a self-transcending oneness exceeds the literal formula’s representational content, but this is adequately treated as contextual interpretation rather than an additional core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
